KW  - BlockTransit-AI
KW  - Geopolitical crisis index (GCI)
KW  - Federated deep Q-network
KW  - Decentralized AI
KW  - Permissioned blockchain
KW  - Multi-agent reinforcement learning
KW  - Edge computing
KW  - Crisis-adaptive transit optimization
KW  - Smart contracts
KW  - Indian metropolitan cities
AB  - The geopolitical disruption of fuel supply to Indian metropolitan cities (with more than 350 million daily commuters) continues to pose a critical risk to the public transit systems because they rely on centralized dispatch architecture and the use of fossil fuels to generate schedules. Although recent studies have focused on AI-driven transit optimization and blockchain-based resource management, no current framework combines real-time geopolitical risk quantification, federated multi-agent reinforcement learning and permissioned smart contract-enforced fuel allocation into one, crisis-adaptive transit control system in a critical way since India is structurally reliant on imported petroleum. In this paper, a new three-level decentralized AI design based on BlockTransit-AI will be proposed to address this gap by integrating permissioned blockchain (Hyperledger Fabric), Federated Deep Q-Networks (FDQN), and edge-computing inference to manage energy-adaptive public transit. This work has four key novelties: (i) a Geopolitical Crisis Index (GCI), which is a synthesis of commodity futures volatility, diplomatic tension indices and supply-chain telemetry, autonomously activates operational policy switching between Normal, Alert, and Crisis modes; (ii) Hyperledger Fabric smart contracts (FuelTokenIssuer, DepotBidManager, CrisisReallocator) to issue CNG, hydrogen, and EV charging resources in a tamper-proof, auditable manner; (iii) a federated MARL architecture that is capable of autonomously rerouting between distributed edge nodes without a centralized coordinate; and (iv) a hysteresis-stabilized mode-transition protocol that prevents operational instability during marginal crisis periods. Delays in passenger waiting times dropped 28.9% and fleet fuel consumption dropped 34.7% as simulations on synthesized transit networks using Delhi, Mumbai and Kolkata as blueprints show that next generation urban transit can be built around scalable, fault-tolerant frameworks.
ER  - 

