KW  - Nuclear power plant
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Physics-informed neural network
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Sustainable energy
AB  - Nuclear power plants (NPPs) generate virtually carbon-free baseload electricity and occupy a strategic role in the global energy transition, yet their potential as a context for sustainable data-driven innovation remains underexplored in the energy analytics literature. This paper presents a comprehensive integrative review grounded in the 2026 IAEA technical report NR-T-1.22 as its primary authoritative source, enriched by 78 peer-reviewed studies (2018–2026) spanning the full NPP lifecycle across sixteen query clusters. The review delivers: (i) a seven-paradigm AI/ML/DS taxonomy with explicit boundary definitions, a hierarchical structure, and regulatory compatibility ratings under IEC TR 63468:2023; (ii) a cross-domain performance evidence synthesis across fourteen application domains, classified under a four-level data-environment scheme (synthetic / simulator / integral test facility / operating plant) that separates laboratory results from genuine field validation; (iii) a six-barrier adoption analysis with quantified mitigation pathways and a mechanism-level account of why the simulator-to-field gap persists; (iv) a reliability-oriented synthesis of uncertainty quantification, false-positive behaviour, and robustness reporting; and (v) a five-layer AI-NPP integration framework applied diagnostically across domains. Reported performance is strong in simulator settings—high simulator-based classification accuracy in anomaly detection and fault diagnosis, large thermal–hydraulic simulation speed-ups, and economically material fuel-optimisation benefits—and fleet-scale field deployments are documented across 68 NPP units in the IAEA annexes. Critically, however, 53 of the 78 peer-reviewed studies (68%) report synthetic or simulator-only results and only 12 (15%) use operating-plant data; the high headline metrics therefore evidence simulator-stage feasibility rather than demonstrated real-world readiness, and the IAEA field figures are practitioner-reported without an independent non-AI baseline. The paper establishes that systematic AI/ML/DS deployment can advance SDG 7 and SDG 13 through four quantified mechanisms—extended plant lifetimes, improved fuel efficiency, reduced unplanned outages, and accelerated advanced-reactor development—while arguing that the binding constraints are regulatory and institutional rather than algorithmic. Ethical dimensions including algorithmic bias, liability allocation, and safety–critical deployment thresholds are addressed with concrete regulatory and operational specifics.
ER  - 

