UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016003226007660
KW  - Advanced process monitoring
KW  - Digital transformation
KW  - Digital twin architectures
KW  - Trustworthy artificial intelligence
KW  - Hybrid modelling
KW  - Industrial analytics
AB  - Advanced process monitoring has evolved from a primarily statistical fault-surveillance activity into a broader digital capability that supports fault detection, diagnosis, soft sensing, root-cause reasoning, and decision support in increasingly complex industrial environments. Although recent research has significantly advanced understanding of probabilistic monitoring, artificial-intelligence-based monitoring, and other specialized methodological streams, the broader computational ecosystem that enables advanced process monitoring to function in practice remains insufficiently synthesized. In particular, existing reviews rarely provide a unified perspective that links plant connectivity, scientific computing, simulation, monitoring platforms, artificial intelligence frameworks, deployment pathways, and lifecycle management within a single coherent digital architecture. This review addresses that gap by reframing advanced process monitoring as a software-enabled systems discipline rather than only a model-development problem. The paper first traces the evolution of the field from statistical process control and multivariate statistical monitoring to deep learning, computational ecosystems, and digital transformation. It then develops a conceptual foundation for computational ecosystems in advanced process monitoring, proposes a six-layer architectural taxonomy, examines cross-layer interworking and integration mechanisms, and introduces a comparative framework for ecosystem evaluation based on functional suitability rather than software popularity alone. Building on this foundation, the review discusses emerging paradigms, including hybrid physics–data analytics, digital twin-centric monitoring, trustworthy artificial intelligence, adaptive cloud-edge monitoring, and generative-artificial-intelligence-assisted engineering workflows, while also identifying persistent barriers such as fragmented toolchains, weak interoperability, lifecycle immaturity, limited benchmarking realism, and insufficient domain-aware software design. Overall, the review argues that the next stage of progress in advanced process monitoring will depend not only on stronger algorithms but on stronger computational ecosystems capable of transforming plant data, process knowledge, and analytical intelligence into reliable, explainable, and sustainable industrial action.
ER  - 
